Glossary
Metabolism: the sum of the chemical reactions that occur in the cell
Metabolic Pathway: A sequential series of chemical reactions in living cells; each reaction is catalyzed by an enzyme
Catabolic Pathway (Catabolism): The process of breaking down compounds into smaller molecules to release energy.
Anabolic Pathway (Anabolism): The process of using energy to build large molecules from smaller molecules
Thermodynamics: The science that studies the transfer and transformation of thermal energy (heat)
First Law of Thermodynamics: Energy cannot be created or destroyed, but it can be transformed from one type into another and transferred from one object to another
Second Law of Thermodynamics: During any process, the universe tends toward disorder
Entropy (S): A measure with disorder
Energy: The capacity to do work
Potential Energy: Stored energy
Kinetic Energy: The energy in motion
Bond Energy: Energy required to break or form a chemical bond
Free energy (G): Energy from a chemical reaction that is available for doing work
Enthalpy (H): The energy contained in a molecule's chemical bonds
Endergonic Reactions: Chemical reactions that require energy
Exergonic Reactions: Chemical reactions that release energy
Catalyst: A substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without itself undergoing any permanent chemical change. A substance that decrease activation energy.
Adenosine Triphosphate (ATP): An organic compound composed of adenosine and three phosphate groups
adenosine diphosphate (ADP): An organic compound composed of adenosine and two phosphate group
Substrate Level Phosphorylation: ATP formation from transferring a phosphate group to ADP
Redox Reaction: A chemical reaction resulting in both an oxidation and a reduction in the atoms involved in the reaction
Oxidation: Any process in which electrons are removed from an atom or ion
Reduction: Any process in which electrons are added to an atom or ion
Electron Carriers: Compounds that pick up electrons from energy rich compounds and donate them to low energy compounds
Nicotinamide Adenine Dinucleotide (NAD): An electron carrier. A coenzyme involved in redox reactions
Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide (FAD): An electron carrier. A coenzyme involved in redox reactions
Heterotrophs: An organisms that feeds on other organisms to obtain chemical energy
Autotrophs: An organism capable of making organic molecules from inorganic sources by photosynthesis
Glucose: A monosaccharide sugar that is a main source of energy and important metabolic substrate for most living things. Chemical formula: C6H12O6
Aerobic Respiration: A catabolic pathway requires oxygen and converts glucose to carbon dioxide and energy.
Glycolysis: First stage of aerobic respiration. This metabolic pathway breaks down glucose into pyruvate
Pyruvate oxidation: Second stage of aerobic respiration, where pyruvate is transported from the cytoplasm to the mitochondria.
Krebs cycle: Third stage of aerobic respiration. This stage is cyclic set of reactions that requires acetyl CoA and oxidizes it to carbon dioxide while regenerating the compound oxaloacetate and transfers energy from these organic molecules to ATP and energy carriers (NADH and FADH2)
Electron transport chain: Fourth stage of aerobic respiration. A series of electron carriers and protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane that accept and donate electrons in a sequential series, resulting in oxidative phosphorylation
Chemiosmosis: A process that uses energy in a hydrogen ion gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane to drive phosphorylation of ADP to ATP
Oxidative Phosphorylation: A process that couples the oxidation of NADH and FADH2 by the electron transport chain with the synthesis of ATP by phosphorylation of ADP
Feedback Control Mechanism: A mechanism or a signal that initiates (or accelerates) or inhibits (or slows down) a process.
Allosteric Binding Site: The location on an enzyme where a molecule that is not a substrate may bind
Metabolic Pathway: A sequential series of chemical reactions in living cells; each reaction is catalyzed by an enzyme
