Pyruvate oxidation: Second stage of aerobic respiration, where pyruvate is transported from the cytoplasm to the mitochondria.
Krebs cycle: Third stage of aerobic respiration. This stage is cyclic set of reactions that requires acetyl CoA and oxidizes it to carbon dioxide while regenerating the compound oxaloacetate and transfers energy from these organic molecules to ATP and energy carriers (NADH and FADH2)
Electron transport chain: Fourth stage of aerobic respiration. A series of electron carriers and protein complexes embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane that accept and donate electrons in a sequential series, resulting in oxidative phosphorylation
Chemiosmosis: A process that uses energy in a hydrogen ion gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane to drive phosphorylation of ADP to ATP
Oxidative Phosphorylation: A process that couples the oxidation of NADH and FADH2 by the electron transport chain with the synthesis of ATP by phosphorylation of ADP
Feedback Control Mechanism: A mechanism or a signal that initiates (or accelerates) or inhibits (or slows down) a process.
Allosteric Binding Site: The location on an enzyme where a molecule that is not a substrate may bind
