Lee is often a common surname in English-speaking countries. Lee was the particular 35th-most-common surname inside England and Wales inside 1990s, and the 24th-most-common in the us during the season 2000 census. There are many distinct origins in the Lee surname. The most prevalent is derived from Old English lēah, that means a meadow or perhaps forest clearing. This developed variously to the surnames Lee, Lea, along with Leigh. The Lees of Shropshire (originally Gary de Lee) were notable because forebears of the particular colonial American Lee family which generated Richard Henry Lee, Robert E. Lee, and Zachary Taylor.
More recently, in Britain, Gypsies have used the surnames being a homophonic translation (via ""leek"") in the Romani surname Purrum, which entails ""onion"". Meanwhile, for the majority more than a new fifth of Lees now arrived at it as China or Korean Us citizens translating numerous surnames furthermore romanized as Li, particularly the one deriving from the Chinese word pertaining to ""plum"" pinyin L; In. Kor. , Lee; S. Kor., Rhee), which has become the most common surnames on earth. In America, Lee is the most common surname for China and Korean Americans and also the second-most-common surname pertaining to Asian and Off-shore Islanders, behind the particular Vietnamese Nguyen. Also, Lee is the third-most-common surname among Chinese Singaporeans.
