Here are some of the other nearby cities and regions around Phoenix and Maricopa County where we are actively buying houses, townhouses, lofts, and condos in: Anthem, Apache Junction, Avondale, Buckeye, Cave Creek, Chandler, El Mirage, Gilbert, Glendale, Goodyear, Mesa, New River, Peoria, Queen Creek, Scottsdale, Sun City, Sun City West, Surprise, Tempe.
The history of the modern day Phoenix begins with one Jack Swilling; he was a Civil War veteran. In 1867, when traveling through Salt River Valley, Jack saw great farming potential in the area, and as at the time, the military was already cultivating the far east near Fort McDowell. In the same year, he formed a community that settled in what is today Phoenix. Lord Darrell, who was one of the Swilling's party original settlers, suggested the name 'Phoenix’ to describe the city as born from the former ruins.
After the end of the Mexican-American War in 1848, Mexico sold its northern part to the US as New Mexico of which the city was a part. On May 4, 1868, the Yavapai County Board of Supervisors encompassed the area as a new town and established the first post office of which Swilling was the first postmaster. When Maricopa County was created after dividing Yavapai, the town later became the County seat of the new Maricopa County.
Today, it is the largest city and state capital of Arizona. According to the 2016 count, the population here was 1,615,000 making it the fifth most populated city in the Country with an area of 517 square miles. The city is anchored in a metropolitan area, which is also known as the Valley of the Sun and forms part of the Salt River Valley. This burgeoning Metropolitan Area is among the fastest growing areas in the US with a population of 4.5 million according to the 2010 census report making it the thirteenth metropolitan area with Glendale, Peoria, Scottsdale, Chandler, Tempe, Mesa, and Gilbert cities.
The city lies in the south-central portion of the State of Arizona in the Sonoran Desert. The topography here is flat other than the mountains in and those surrounding the city. The city experiences the hot desert climate of short mild winters and long hot summers typically because it lies in the Sonoran Desert. Desert climate always undergo drastic temperature of fluctuations in the night and day, but due to the urban heat Island, it does not have the extreme variations. The area experiences an average of 27 degrees Fahrenheit temperature in the winters and 104 degrees Fahrenheit in the summer with a mean precipitation of 189mm throughout the year.
On the outskirts of the city, there are large tracts of irrigated land and Salt River runs westward through the city. Agriculture and natural resources remain the drivers of the economy served by Phx Sky Harbor International Airport that is the tenth busiest airport in the country. Due to the climate and natural beauty of the city, it has numerous outdoor recreational activities. Some of the attractions include the Botanical Gardens, the PHX Zoo, and South Mountain Park among others.
The city is home to many major professional sports franchises. The franchises include Suns NBA team and Mercury of the WBNA who both play at Talking Stick Resort Arena. The Arizona Diamondbacks of Major League Baseball, Arizona Cardinals of NFL, and Arizona Coyotes of National Hockey League also come from the city. The four-year public universities that have campuses in the city include Arizona State University, the University of Arizona, and Northern Arizona University.
