Image: Hangar 5 at RAF Waddington geograph.org.uk 4061277"
when did oral storytelling most likely first develop,"['https://greendreamcompany.com/author/leon-van-rijckevorsel/', 'https://jamesdugan.com/blog/embracing-heritage-with-storytelling', 'https://www.strong-bridge-envision.com/five-traits-great-storytellers/', 'https://interactiveme.com/2012/03/qa-with-yahki-social-storytelling-platform-1-of-2/', 'https://www.artimpactusa.org/hubert-jackson-light.html', 'https://soydanbay.com/2011/06/30/branding-through-storytelling/']","It is likely that oral storytelling has been around as long as human language. Storytelling fulfills the need for human beings to cast their experiences in narrative form. Our ancestors probably gathered around the evening fires and expressed their fears, their beliefs and their heroism through oral narratives. This long tradition of storytelling is evident in ancient cultures such as the Australian Aborigines. Community storytelling offered the security of explanation; how life and its many forms began and why things happen, as well as entertainment and enchantment. Communities were strengthened and maintained through stories that connected the present, the past and the future.","Skip to content
