Energy stored in magnetic fields
In order to create a magnetic field we need to do work to establish a free current. If one were to ask how much energy does it take to create a specific magnetic field using a particular free current then one needs to distinguish between the free and the bound currents. It is the free current that one is ""pushing"" on. The bound currents are freeloaders. They create a magnetic field that the free current has to work against without doing any of the work. If one was to calculate the energy of creating a magnetic field we need to have a way of separating out the free current. The magnetic cannot be used to determine this free current since does not distinguish between bound and free current.
