where did the easter egg hunt come from,"['http://newsletters.santacruzparent.com/Newsletters/view/April-2-2015-,503.html', 'https://ioanabarbu.com/2018/04/07/easter-gratitude-traditions-and-wishes/', 'https://www.myjournalcourier.com/news/article/Easter-tradition-both-old-new-12638775.php', 'http://www.roses2rainbows.com/search?q=easter', 'https://www.english-in-chester.co.uk/student-life/this-month-at-eic/?flavour=mobile', 'https://gorhino.co.za/easter-egg-hunt-traditions/', 'http://mplsobserver.com/2017/04/22/to-do-wunderlists-successor-is-available-for-public-preview.html']","The egg was a symbol of the rebirth of the earth in pre-Christian celebrations of spring. However, the Easter egg itself was defined by early Christians as an Easter symbol of the resurrection of Jesus: the egg symbol was likened to the tomb from which Christ arose. Lizette Larson-Miller, a professor with the Graduate Theological Union of Berkeley, traces the specific custom of the Easter egg hunt to the Protestant Christian Reformer Martin Luther, stating ""We know that Martin Luther had Easter egg hunts where the men hid the eggs for the women and children, and it probably has this connection back to this idea of eggs being the tomb."" At least since the 17th century the idea of the Easter Bunny to bring the Easter eggs has been known. The novelty of the introduction of Easter egg hunts into England is evidenced by A.E. Housman's inaugural lecture as Professor of Latin at University College, London in 1892, in which he said, ""In Germany at Easter time they hide coloured eggs about the house and garden that the children may amuse themselves in discovering them.""","404 Not Found 404 Not Found Please forward this error screen to newsletters.santacruzparent.com's WebMaster. The server cannot find the requested page: newsletters.santacruzparent.com/Newsletters/view/April-2-2015-,503.html (port 80) [SEP_DOC] Dozens of children eagerly collected more than 6,000 multicolored, candy-filled Easter eggs that were strewn Saturday across the front lawn of a South Jacksonville church.
“I think it’s fantastic that they’re doing something like this for the kids,” said Juanita Mann of Jacksonville, who brought her 4-year-old granddaughter, Josalen Schofield, to First Christian Church’s inaugural Easter egg hunt.
“We saw a need for an Easter egg hunt in the community, and First Christian Church wanted to fill that need,” said Tina Ehlenbeck, who coordinated the event with Brian VanArsdale.
Advertisement
Article continues below this ad
The Easter egg hunt had three divisions: birth to 3 years; kindergarten-second grades; and third-sixth grades. Multiple drawings for prizes were held for each of the divisions. The largest prizes were three $100 gift cards to local businesses. Sponsors were McDonald’s, Family Video, Bowl Inn, Applebee’s and Skateland.
“I think it’s important for the kids in the community to have an event just for themselves,” Ehlenbeck said. “Next year we hope to have the Easter egg hunt in a local park and have more than 20,000 eggs,” VanArsdale said.
The Easter egg was defined by early Christians as an Easter symbol of the resurrection of Jesus: the egg symbol was likened to the empty tomb after Christ arose from the dead, according to Wikipedia, with the egg itself resembling the stone of the tomb and birds hatching from eggs with life. The idea of the Easter Bunny bringing Easter eggs has been around since at least the 17th century."
