There is a tendency for the resistance of many electrically conducting materials to change with temperature. The effect was described to the Royal Society of Great Britain in a lecture given by the German-born engineer Carl Willhelm Siemens (1823-1883) in 1871. If the resistance of a conductor can be shown to vary with temperature in a predictable way, such a conductor can be used to obtain accurate and consistent temperature measurements. The core component of a resistance temperature detector (RTD) is thus a conductor, usually made of platinum, whose resistance varies in a precise and linear way with temperature. The main categories of RTD construction are wire-wound, coiled element, and thin film.
In a wire-wound RTD, the wire is wound around a glass or ceramic core and housed within a protective sheath. The diameter of the core will determine the extent to which the wire is allowed to expand as its temperature increases. If the wire is tightly constrained it will be more mechanically stable, but may suffer strain effects as it expands that can affect the accuracy of temperature readings. Wire-wound RTDs can be used at temperatures of up to about 660 °C.
A wire-wound RTD
A coiled element RTD has a wire coil that looks a bit like the filament in a light bulb, housed within a ceramic tube. The ceramic tube holds the coil in place while allowing it the freedom to expand and contract without incurring any strain effects. A fine ceramic powder is packed around the coil, allowing it to move but ensuring that it has good thermal contact with whatever process is being measured. Coiled element RTDs can be used at temperatures of up to about 850 °C.
A coiled element RTD
A thin film RTD, as its name suggests, has an element that is formed by depositing a very thin layer of resistive material (usually platinum) on a ceramic substrate. The element is then encapsulated with a protective epoxy resin or glass coating. There is a special area on the substrate called the resistance trim area where the resistive material is deposited to form a sort of grid pattern. The total resistance of the thin film RTD can be increased by cutting one or more of the traces in the grid to increase the overall length of the circuit.
Thin film RTDs are generally smaller, cheaper and more durable than wire-wound or coiled element RTDs. They also respond faster to changes in temperature, and are not as sensitive to vibration. On the down side, thin film RTDs are not as mechanically stable as wire-wound or coiled element RTDs, and until relatively recently could not be used at temperatures much above 600 °C because of strain effects caused by the different expansion rates of the element and its substrate. They also tended to be less accurate than other types of RTD.
Recent improvements in manufacturing techniques, together with the use of alternative materials for encapsulation, have enabled the production of thin film RTDs that can be used at temperatures of up to about 900 °C. Accuracy has also been improved to the extent that thin film RTDs are now on a par with their wire-wound counterparts.
A thin film RTD
A circuit connected to the RTD supplies it with a constant current, and monitors the voltage drop across it to detect any changes in resistance. The lead wires that supply the RTD with current must be insulated to protect them against mechanical damage and hostile chemicals in the environment in which the RTD must operate. They must also be compatible with the resistance wire in the RTD (known as the sensing wire) to ensure that no unwanted electro-magnetic fields are generated that would affect the accuracy of the measurements.
Variations in the supply voltage, and the resistance in the RTD's lead wires, can also affect the accuracy of the readings obtained. The circuitry used with the RTD is therefore designed to eliminate, or at least minimise as far as possible, the errors induced by such factors.
Platinum is widely used for making RTDs because it is a noble metal, which means it is highly resistant to corrosion and oxidation in moist air, and because its behaviour remains stable over a wide range of temperatures. At least one source gives the useful temperature range of platinum as -272.5 °C to 961.78 °C (the latter being the freezing point of silver). Platinum is used in the sensors that define the International Temperature Standard, ITS-90 (see above). It is also used in the construction of laboratory grade RTDs.
Other materials used include copper, nickel and carbon. Nickel is cheaper than platinum but its resistance becomes non-linear at temperatures above 300 °C. Copper is cheaper still, and has a very linear resistance-to-temperature relationship, but oxidises at temperatures in excess of 150 °C. Carbon resistor RTDs are cheap to manufacture and are particularly suited to extremely low temperature applications (from -173 °C down to -273 °C).
One of the most important characteristics of an RTD is its temperature coefficient of resistance (TCR), which defines the way in which the RTD's resistance varies with temperature. This value is denoted by the Greek lower case letter alpha (α), and is usually measured over a temperature range of 0 °C to 100 °C, with the sensing wire frequently being manufactured to have a nominal resistance of one hundred ohms (100Ω) at 0 °C. The resistance of typical platinum RTDs over this temperature range will increase by 0.385 ohms for each one degree Celsius increase in temperature. The value for alpha can be calculated as:
where R0 is the resistance at 0 °C and R100 is the resistance at 100 °C.
For temperatures outside the range 0 °C to 100 °C, the temperature coefficient of an RTD may vary slightly. Although this variance is usually very small, it is good practice to calibrate the RTD at temperatures other than 0 °C and 100 °C in order to ensure that the temperatures recorded remain accurate for the entire range of temperatures over which the device will be used. RTDs are given a tolerance class according to how accurate they are.
