Credit for the discovery that led to the thermistor (a portmanteau of thermal and resistor) goes to the English scientist Michael Faraday (1791-1867). Faraday discovered that the resistance of silver sulphide decreases markedly as its temperature increases. Silver sulphide is a semiconductor material (a chemical element or compound that conducts electricity, but only under certain conditions). Despite this discovery, the use of semiconductor materials to produce temperature measurement devices would not occur until 1930, when American inventor Samuel Ruben (1900-1988) invented the first commercially viable thermistor.
A thermistor works in a similar way to an RTD. Like an RTD, it is constructed of a material (in this case a ceramic semiconductor) whose resistance varies with temperature. And, like an RTD, it must be connected to circuitry that supplies it with a constant current and monitors the voltage drop across it to detect any changes in resistance.
Unlike RTDs, the resistance of a typical* thermistor actually decreases as temperature increases. This is because the negatively charged particles in the material (electrons) will have more energy. As a result, some electrons break the bonds that tie them to a particular atom and can move freely through the semiconductor material. The ""hole"" left behind by the electron carries a positive charge. Overall, this results in the semiconductor material having more ""charge carriers"" (electrons and holes), allowing electric current to flow through it more easily.
* Thermistors can have either a negative temperature coefficient (NTC thermistors – resistance decreases with temperature) or a positive temperature coefficient (PTC thermistors - resistance increases with temperature). The thermistors used to measure temperature are almost always NTC thermistors.
By comparison with the metals used in RTDs, the resistivity of semiconductor materials is far more sensitive to changes in temperature (a small change in temperature produces a significant change in resistance), so it is easier to detect relatively small temperature changes with a thermistor than with an RTD. Thermistors also tend to have very high resistances compared to RTDs. This means they can be used to monitor temperatures in remote locations, because the resistance in the lead wires is negligible by comparison, even over relatively long distances.
On the down side, the relationship between resistance and temperature for thermistors is highly non-linear, and their use tends to be restricted to a much narrower range of temperatures. The resistance of a semiconductor material can also change over time, especially if it is subjected to high temperatures for long periods. This effect is known as drift.
Depending on the materials from which they are constructed and the production methods used to produce them, thermistors can be used to measure temperatures from around minus fifty degrees Celsius up to about three hundred degrees Celsius (-50 °C - 300 °C). Above 150 °C, however, they tend to become less sensitive to small temperature variations. Thermistors tend to be used for relatively low temperature applications in which relatively small changes in temperature may be significant. They are typically used in the thermostats used to control heating and environmental control systems, and can be found in a multitude of consumer appliances, such as toasters, refrigerators and hair dryers. They are also widely used in the automotive industry, and in the food handling and processing industry.
The resistance value of a thermistor is usually referenced at 25 °C, and this value is known as the thermistor's R25 value. Typical R25 values range from one hundred ohms up to one hundred kilohms (100 Ω - 100 kΩ), although thermistors with R25 values as low as ten ohms (10 Ω) or as high as forty megohms (40 MΩ) may be encountered.
If we plot a graph of the relationship between resistance and temperature for a particular thermistor, we will get the characteristic resistance versus temperature curve for that thermistor. This curve (known as the R/T curve) represents a non-linear, negative exponential function that can be modelled using various equations. The best known of these equations is the Steinhart-Hart equation, which looks like this:
1/T = A + B(ln R) + C(ln R) 3
The equation involves significant computational overhead, but is widely used because it provides a very accurate approximation of the actual temperature, and works for the entire temperature range over which the sensor is used. T is the temperature in kelvins, and R is the resistance at that temperature. A, B and C are the Steinhart-Hart coefficients, which will vary depending on the type of thermistor. The Steinhart-Hart coefficients can be found by accurately measuring the thermistor's resistance at three known temperatures, and then solving a set of three simultaneous equations. There is usually no need to do this, however, as the Steinhart-Hart coefficients for a particular thermistor are published by the thermistor manufacturer.
Most NTC thermistors are manufactured using metal oxide powders of various kinds, including the oxides of cobalt, copper, iron, manganese and nickel. The thermistor's R25 value and R/T curve will largely be determined by the particular metal oxide used. The choice of materials will depend on the application - materials that have a very high resistance are better suited for high temperature applications, whereas other materials may exhibit greater long-term stability. Although thermistors can be produced in many different formats, most take the form of a disc, bead or chip.
Disc thermistors are made by mixing a metal oxide powder with a suitable binding agent and compressing it under very high pressure in a die. The resulting disk-shaped pellet is then fired in a high temperature kiln to form a small ceramic disc, typically 1.3 mm to 2.5 mm in diameter. A thick-film electrode material (typically silver) is deposited on each side of the disk to form the contacts for the lead wires. A protective coating is then applied to the disc to protect it from mechanical damage and potentially harmful environments. The materials commonly used include glass (for higher temperature applications), epoxy resin, and phenolic resin (for those interested in such things, phenolic resin is the stuff used to manufacture most snooker, pool and billiard balls).
Bead thermistors are produced in much the same way as disc thermistors but are formed into smaller, bead-shaped pellets (as the name suggests!) before firing. Bead thermistors are significantly smaller than disk thermistors (typically between 0.25 mm and 1.5 mm in diameter), and are usually given a protective coating of glass.
Chip thermistors are square or rectangular in shape, with side lengths of between 1 mm and 2.5 mm. They are manufactured using a process known as tape casting, in which a mixture of a ceramic material and a resin binding agent is extruded onto a flat surface to form a thin sheet. Once dry, the sheet can be cut or punched to the required shape and size before being fired and coated with phenolic or epoxy resin. Chip thermistors are ideal for use as surface mounted temperature sensors on printed circuit boards.
The main thermistor types
Thermistors must be supplied with electrical power in order to work, and this power will be converted to heat. This has the potential to raise the temperature of the thermistor and introduce errors into the temperature readings obtained. The degree to which a thermistor can dissipate power to its surroundings is called its dissipation constant, and will relate to the size of the thermistor (the smaller a thermistor element is, the more likely it is to experience self-heating). The problem can be mitigated by minimising the amount of current flowing through the thermistor and ensuring there is a good thermal pathway between the thermistor element and its surroundings.
The relatively small size of glass bead thermistors means that they respond very quickly to changes in temperature, but also limits their ability to dissipate power. Disc and chip thermistors tend to be larger, which means they do not respond to temperature changes as quickly as bead thermistors, but are better able to dissipate power.
Generally speaking, there is a trade-off between the degree to which a thermistor provides accurate results (referred to as its tolerance) and the speed with which it responds to changes in temperature. Modern disc and chip thermistors typically have an accuracy of between ± 0.1 °C and ± 0.2 °C for the temperature range 0 °C to 100 °C. Whilst it is possible to produce bead thermistors with comparable tolerances, their cost will be significantly higher.
Many industrial applications require temperature sensors that can function in a hostile environment. Although thermistor elements can be inserted directly into an operating environment, they are vulnerable to corrosion, oxidation and mechanical damage. Consequently, thermistor elements are often packaged inside a protective housing or probe, typically consisting of a closed-ended metal tube. The probe is usually packed with a thermally conducting material (e.g. epoxy resin) to ensure good thermal conduction between the thermistor element and the process being measured.
