An infrared thermometer measures temperature without making direct contact with the object or substance to be measured by measuring the infrared radiation emitted by that object or substance. Infrared radiation (sometimes called blackbody radiation) is invisible radiant energy, first discovered in 1800 by the German-born British astronomer Frederick William Herschel (1738-1822). It is part of the electromagnetic spectrum, with wavelengths longer than those of visible light. Every form of matter with a temperature above absolute zero emits infrared radiation. The amount of infrared radiation emitted increases with temperature.
This high-temperature infrared thermometer can monitor temperatures of up to 900 °C
Herschel's discovery paved the way for a new kind of temperature measurement device that could be used at a distance from the target. Such devices eliminated the risk of damage or contamination due to contact with the target, and could safely be used to measure very high temperatures. Early infrared thermometers were heavy, bulky and difficult to operate. Their modern counterparts are typically lightweight hand-held devices that are easy to operate while at the same time offering a range of sophisticated features.
A typical infrared thermometer uses a lens to focus the received infrared light onto a detector, consisting of a thermopile (several serially-connected thermocouples - see above), that converts thermal energy into an electrical signal. The signal is then interpreted by an electronic circuit, which displays the temperature on an electronic display panel. Because the surfaces of different materials have different levels of emissivity, the instrument may need to be calibrated for use in a particular application. Reflective surfaces have a lower emissivity than non-reflective surfaces, and many infrared thermometers have an adjustable emissivity setting to take account of this.
The most commonly used type of infrared thermometer is a spot infrared thermometer which, as the name suggests, measures the temperature of a relatively small circular area on a surface. The actual size of the spot is determined by the distance-to-spot ratio (or D:S ratio) which is the ratio of the distance between the thermometer and the surface to be measured and the diameter of the spot. For a D:S ratio of 10:1, for example, the diameter of the spot will be one-tenth of the distance between the thermometer and the surface to be measured.
Most handheld devices have a fixed D:S ratio, which means that there is an optimal distance for temperature measurements. Accurate measurements can be obtained at other distances, but the size of the spot will vary accordingly. Care must be taken to ensure that the target completely fills the area covered by the spot. If not, infrared emissions from background objects may adversely affect the accuracy of the measurement. Many infrared thermometers are equipped with a laser pointer to make it easier for the user to centre the spot on the target area.
Infrared thermometers have many applications. They can be used to measure the temperature of objects affected by strong magnetic fields, or contained within a controlled, atmosphere, or subject to hazardous conditions. Fast response times mean they can be used to measure the temperature of moving objects (on a conveyor belt or assembly line, for example). They can also be used in high-temperature applications where the temperatures involved preclude the use of contact sensors, or in corrosive environments where contact sensors would be easily damaged, or to monitor surfaces that could become contaminated by contact with a physical sensor, or where the presence of a contact sensor could introduce a significant temperature gradient on the surface to be measured.
Infrared thermometers are typically used to monitor machinery and electrical equipment for hot spots, and to monitor temperatures in industrial processes. As well as handheld devices, infrared thermometers can be installed at fixed points in chemical and manufacturing plants to monitor temperature-critical operations. These devices are often controlled remotely using video monitoring equipment, and send their data to a control station. In the field of medicine, infrared thermometers may be used to measure the temperature of hospital patients without touching them (for example, under quarantine conditions). In recent years, airport workers have used infrared thermometers to screen passengers arriving from countries suffering from outbreaks of infectious diseases, such as the Ebola virus.
