- Clayton Kershaw on Twitter [SEP_DOC] NFL Mock Draft 2025 | NFL Draft Prediction - Lines.com Close the Ad NFL Odds Props Picks Parlays Futures Teams Standings Stats Schedule Injuries Drafts How to bet Home / NFL / 2025 NFL Draft: Complete Overview 2025 NFL Draft: Complete Overview The 2025 NFL Draft took place April 24-26, 2025, delivering one of the most talent-rich classes in recent memory with multiple award winners, elite two-way prospects, and exceptional positional depth. Tennessee Titans selected first overall with Miami quarterback Cam Ward, the 2024 Davey O’Brien Award winner, while Jacksonville made history at Pick 2 by selecting Heisman Trophy winner Travis Hunter from Colorado, marking the first primarily defensive player drafted in the top two since 1997. Recommended Read: How many rounds are in the NFL draft? Top 10 selections in NFL 2025 Draft The 2025 draft’s opening selections established several historic precedents and franchise-altering decisions: Picks 1-5: Elite talent concentration Tennessee secured Cam Ward (QB, Miami) at Pick 1, acquiring the nation’s most pro-ready quarterback who posted exceptional college production with Davey O’Brien Award credentials. Jacksonville shocked analysts by selecting Travis Hunter (CB/WR, Colorado) at Pick 2, the 2024 Heisman Trophy winner whose unprecedented two-way dominance (playing 1,287 defensive snaps and 542 offensive snaps) created the draft’s most unique evaluation challenge since Charles Woodson in 1998. The New York Giants addressed their pass rush with Abdul Carter (DE, Penn State) at Pick 3, landing an elite edge defender with 13 sacks and 21 tackles for loss who projects as immediate three-down impact player. New England secured Will Campbell (OT, LSU) at Pick 4, the draft’s best offensive tackle who allowed just 3 sacks across 847 pass blocking snaps during his college career. Cleveland completed the top five with Mason Graham (DT, Michigan), an interior disruptor with 8.5 sacks and elite run defense grades (84.7 from Pro Football Focus). Picks 6-10: Positional value and scheme fits Las Vegas made the draft’s biggest positional reach by selecting Ashton Jeanty (RB, Boise State) at Pick 6, the 2024 Maxwell Award winner who rushed for 2,288 yards and 29 touchdowns. While running back value rarely justifies top-10 investment, Jeanty’s generational production (averaging 8.2 yards per carry) convinced Raiders management his immediate impact outweighed positional economics concerns. The New York Jets addressed offensive line needs with Armand Membou (OT, Missouri) at Pick 7, while Carolina secured the draft’s best wide receiver in Tetairoa McMillan (WR, Arizona) at Pick 8. His 6’5″, 212-pound frame combined with 4.42-second 40-yard dash created matchup problems scouts compared to Calvin Johnson. New Orleans grabbed Kelvin Banks Jr. (OT, Texas) at Pick 9, the 2024 Lombardi Award winner who allowed zero sacks during his final college season, and Chicago completed the top 10 with Colston Loveland (TE, Michigan), addressing their tight end void with college football’s most complete receiving tight end. Award winners dominate first round The 2025 draft featured unprecedented concentration of award-winning talent throughout Round 1, with nine major college football award winners selected in the first 32 picks: Cam Ward (Pick 1): 2024 Davey O’Brien Award (nation’s best quarterback) Travis Hunter (Pick 2): 2024 Heisman Trophy (college football’s most outstanding player) Ashton Jeanty (Pick 6): 2024 Maxwell Award (college football’s player of the year) Kelvin Banks Jr. (Pick 9): 2024 Lombardi Award (nation’s best lineman) Tyler Warren (Pick 14): 2024 John Mackey Award (nation’s best tight end) Jalon Walker (Pick 15): 2024 Dick Butkus Award (nation’s best linebacker) Jahdae Barron (Pick 20): 2024 Jim Thorpe Award (nation’s best defensive back) Donovan Ezeiruaku (Pick 44, Round 2): 2024 Ted Hendricks Award (nation’s best defensive end) Kyle Kennard (Pick 125, Round 4): 2024 Bronko Nagurski Trophy (nation’s best defensive player) This concentration of award winners represents the highest first-round total since 2018’s draft class, validating pre-draft consensus that 2025 offered exceptional depth across multiple position groups. Total 2025 NFL Draft Statistics: Total selections: 257 picks across 7 rounds Trades involving draft picks: 47 trades (including future picks) International players selected: 11 (Canada, Germany, Australia represented) FCS/Division II players: 23 total selections Compensatory picks awarded: 38 selections Record contracts (rookie scale): Pick 1 ($41.3M fully guaranteed over 4 years) The 2025 NFL Draft established new benchmarks for talent concentration, betting engagement, and media coverage while providing 32 franchises with immediate roster upgrades and long-term competitive foundations. Affiliate Disclosure: Lines may receive advertising commissions for visits to a sportsbook or betting site. Related Articles Best Mobile Casino Apps 2026: Top Sweepstakes Casinos for "
who was the last british monarch born abroad,"['https://www.rookebooks.com/product?prod_id=38443', 'http://coinparade.co.uk/1729-five-guinea-eic/', 'https://trakia-conference.eu/hessenpokal.html', 'https://wikivisually.com/wiki/1728', 'https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2016/04/outlander-season-2-history', 'https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/learn/story-of-england/georgians/', 'https://mlbft.co.uk/last-king-in-battle/']","George was the last British monarch born outside Great Britain: he was born and brought up in northern Germany. His grandmother, Sophia of Hanover, became second in line to the British throne after about fifty Catholics higher in line were excluded by the Act of Settlement 1701 and the Acts of Union 1707, which restricted the succession to Protestants. After the deaths of Sophia and Anne, Queen of Great Britain, in 1714, his father George I, Elector of Hanover, inherited the British throne. In the first years of his father's reign as king, George was associated with opposition politicians, until they rejoined the governing party in 1720.","1729 Five Guinea EIC - George II
The 1729 Five Guinea EIC is a British Gold Coin minted under the reign of George II from gold bought from the East India Company.
Guineas got their name because the gold used to mint them came from Guinea in West Africa. Over the years gold came from other sources too and the Royal Mint started a practice of acknowledging the source of the gold by writing it under the King's head. 'LIMA' was used for gold captured from Spanish ships and 'E.I.C' was gold that came from the East India Company. The Five Guinea coin was the largest of the coins that bear the E.I.C. mark and although the actual mintage is not known it is a classed as a rare coin.
The East India Company was also known as the British East India Company and informally as the John Company. It was founded in London in 1600 by John Watts and George White as a company to trade in the East Indies (today's Southeast Asia) but it traded with India, China and most of the Indian subcontinent.
The East India Company's might grew to account to half the World's trade, with commodies like cotton, silk, salt, tea, saltpetre and opium. EIC helped build the British Empire in India, and was aggressive with it. By 1800, it had a private army of 260,000 men, which was twice as big as the British Army.
EIC effectively ruled India from 1757 until 1858, not always with financial gains. In 1857 the Indians had had enough and the Indian Rebellion of 1857, although not greatly sucessful in its own right, led to the British Government blaming and then nationalising the East India Company and bringing India under direct rule with a British Raj. Their armies were merged into the British Army and the company was officially dissolved in 1874.
