who claimed that he brought peace in our time,"['https://www.surreyguildofcraftsmen.co.uk/blog/tag/RBL', 'http://ruth-morris.info/news.php?action=show&id=12456', 'https://wikimili.com/en/Neville_Chamberlain', 'https://revolution2-0.org/never-forget-really-ep-61/', 'https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco/wiki/Benjamin_Disraeli.html']","""Peace for our time"" was a declaration made by British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain in his 30 September 1938 speech concerning the Munich Agreement and the Anglo-German Declaration. The phrase echoed Benjamin Disraeli, who, upon returning from the Congress of Berlin in 1878, stated, ""I have returned from Germany with peace for our time"". It is primarily remembered for its ironic value: less than a year after the agreement, Hitler's continued aggression and his invasion of Poland was followed by declarations of war on Germany by France and the United Kingdom.",who claimed that he brought peace in our time
what does it mean to be more viscous,"['https://testbook.com/blog/hydraulics-ssc-je-civil-quiz-2/', 'http://easonhan.info/?page_id=20780', 'http://www.epakelectronics.com/test_measurement_equipment.htm', 'http://www.nitrio.com/apps/Viscosity_Conversion_iOS_App.html', 'https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/amp/dynamic-absolute-kinematic-viscosity-d_412.html', 'http://buy-winstrol.me/post/process-flow-diagram-tomato-ketchup', 'https://www.cannoninstrument.com/en/content/viscosity-and-rheology-classroom']","The viscosity of a fluid is the measure of its resistance to gradual deformation by shear stress or tensile stress. For liquids, it corresponds to the informal concept of ""thickness"": for example, honey has a higher viscosity than water.","Hydraulics - SSC JE Civil Quiz 2 Home » Blog Hydraulics – SSC JE Civil Quiz 2 Kabya Srivastava | Updated: Dec 14, 2016 21:27 IST 0 Save Download Post as PDF With the Staff Selection Commission’s Junior Engineer Paper I close at hand, you must be hard at work studying. Here we present students of SSC JE CE (Civil) quizzes for practice and revision. SSC JE (CE) Quiz 2 deals with the topic of Hydraulics. Table of Contents Hydraulics – SSC JE CE Quiz 2SolutionSolutionSolutionSolutionSolutionSolutionSolutionSolutionSolutionSolution Hydraulics – SSC JE CE Quiz 2 Que. 1 The stream function in a two-dimensional flow field is given by ψ = x2 – y2. Then the magnitude of velocity at point (1, 1) is 1. 2 2. \(2\sqrt 2\) 3. 4 4. 8 Skip Solution Answer: Concept: Stream Function (ψ): \(v = \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial x}};u = – \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial y}}\) Velocity Potential Function(ϕ): \(u = – \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial x}};v = – \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial y}};w = – \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial z}}\) Calculation: \(\begin{array}{l} \psi = {x^2} – {y^2} ;u =- \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial y}} =- \frac{\partial }{{\partial y}}\left[ {{x^2} – {y^2}} \right] \end{array}\) u = 2y \(v = \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial x}} = \frac{\partial }{{\partial x}}\left[ {{x^2} – {y^2}} \right]\) v = 2x Resultant velocity at (1, 1) \(\begin{array}{l} V= \sqrt {{u^2} + {v^2}} = \sqrt {{{\left( { 2y} \right)}^2} + {{\left( { 2x} \right)}^2}} = \sqrt {4{y^2} + 4{x^2}} = \sqrt {4 + 4} = 2\sqrt 2 \;units \end{array}\) Attempt these questions in our app. Download now Que. 2 For an irrotational flow, the velocity potential lines and the streamlines are always 1. Parallel to each other 2. Coplanar 3. Orthogonal to each other 4. Inclined to the horizontal Skip Solution Answer: Explanation: Streamline: \(\begin{array}{l} u = \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial y}},v = – \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial x}} \end{array}\) \(d\psi = \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial x}}dx + \frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial y}}dy = 0 \) \(\frac{{dy}}{{dx}} = – \frac{{\frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial x}}}}{{\frac{{\partial \psi }}{{\partial y}}}} = \frac{v}{u}\) Potential line: \(u = \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial x}},v = \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial y}}\) \(d\phi = \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial x}}dx + \frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial y}}dy = 0\) \(\frac{{dy}}{{dx}} = – \frac{{\frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial x}}}}{{\frac{{\partial \phi }}{{\partial y}}}} = – \frac{u}{v}\) The slope of the velocity potential \(= {\left( {\frac{{dy}}{{dx}}} \right)_1} = – \frac{u}{v}\) The slope of the stream-line \({\left( {\frac{{dy}}{{dx}}} \right)_2} = \frac{v}{u}\) \({\left( {\frac{{dy}}{{dx}}} \right)_1} \times {\left( {\frac{{dy}}{{dx}}} \right)_2} = – \frac{u}{v} \times \frac{v}{u} = – 1\) Hence, they are orthogonal to each other (∵ m1m2 = -1) Attempt these questions in our app. Download now Que. 3 An oil of kinematic viscosity 0.5 stoke is flowing through a pipe of 5 cm diameter. The flow through the pipe becomes critical at velocity of 1. 0.2 m/s 2. 2 m/s 3. 2.5 m/s 4. 4 m/s Skip Solution Answer: Concept: The critical value of reynolds number for a pipe flow is 2000 Given v = 0.5 stoke = 0.5 × 10-4 m2/s D = 5 × 10-2 m Critical value of Reynolds number = 2000 \(\begin{array}{l} Re = \frac{{VD}}{v}\\ 2000 = \frac{{V \times 5 \times {{10}^{ – 2}}}}{{0.5 \times 10 – 4}} \end{array}\) ⇒ V = 2 m/s Attempt these questions in our app. Download now Que. 4 Kinematic similarity between Model and prototype is the similarity of 1. Shape 2. Discharge / Motion 3. Stream 4. Forces Skip Solution Answer: Concepts: Kinematic Similarity: Kinematic similarity means the similarity of motion between model and prototype. If the ratio of velocity and acceleration at the corresponding points in the model and at the corresponding points in the prototype are the same. Other types of similarities exist between the model and prototype are: Geometric Similarity: When the ratio of all corresponding linear dimensions in the model and prototype are equal. Dynamic Similarity: This similarity exists when if the ration of forces between the model and prototype is equal. For pipe flow, the dynamic similarity will be obtained if the Reynolds number in the model and prototype are equal. (Re)Model = (Re)Prototype \(\frac{{{\rho _m}{V_m}{D_m}}}{{{\mu _m}}} = \frac{{{\rho _p}{V_p}{D_p}}}{{{\mu _p}}}\) \(\frac{{{V_m}{D_m}}}{{{\nu _m}}} = \frac{{{V_p}{D_p}}}{{{\nu _p}}}\) Attempt these questions in our app. Download now Que. 5 In a rectangular channel the depth of flow is 2 m and the specific energy at that section is 2.7 m. The flow is: 1. Sub-critical 2. Super-critical 3. Critical 4. Not possible Skip Solution Answer: Calculation: Given, Specific energy (E) = 2.7, Depth of flow (y) = 2 m Specific Energy at a section is given by, E = y + v2/2g ⇒ \(E = y + \frac{{{q^2}}}{{2g \times {y^2}}}\) \(2.7 = 2 + \frac{{{q^2}}}{{2 \times 9.81 \times {2^2}}}\) q = 7.41 m2/s  [SEP_DOC] We offer a range of test and measurement equipment for production, manufacturing and printing. See below for further information.
