the special relationship between the us and great britain,"['http://xahyzecawojozog.ga/7c2f46d95.html', 'http://www.iran-daily.com/export/html/208598?module=news', 'https://globalinfoblog.com/tag/constitution/', 'https://alphahistory.com/coldwar/cold-war-glossary-a-l/', 'https://www.jfedgmw.org/the-corridor/leadership-failures-and-leadership-vision-part-2', 'http://v-nep.org/in-the-classroom/us-military-history/star-telegram/', 'https://essayshark.com/blog/history-research-paper-sample/']","The Special Relationship is an unofficial term for the political, diplomatic, cultural, economic, military, and historical relations between the United Kingdom and the United States. It was used in a 1946 speech by Winston Churchill. The two nations have been close allies in many conflicts in the 20th and 21st centuries, including World Wars I and II, the Korean War, the Cold War, the Gulf War, and the War on Terror. Although the UK and US have close relationships with many other nations, the level of cooperation between them in economic activity, trade and commerce, military planning, execution of military operations, nuclear weapons technology, and intelligence sharing has been described as ""unparalleled"" among major powers.",the special relationship between the us and great britain
what are homologus organs how do they indicate evolution,"['http://zippo-lighter.info/news.php?subaction=showfull&id=36141', 'http://genome101.com/Homologies', 'http://wiki-offline.jakearchibald.com/wiki/Homology_(biology)', 'https://sciencetrends.com/homologous-structures-definition-and-examples/', 'https://dlc.dcccd.edu/html5/biology/lesson1/Bio01_ProgressCheck.html', 'https://twobrothersweblog.com/2016/12/21/qa-why-would-an-infinite-creator-employ-the-same-designs/', 'http://mujahidsufi.blogspot.com/2008/05/deceit-of-evolution-theoryfrom.html']","In biology, homology is the existence of shared ancestry between a pair of structures, or genes, in different taxa. A common example of homologous structures is the forelimbs of vertebrates, where the wings of bats, the arms of primates, the front flippers of whales and the forelegs of dogs and horses are all derived from the same ancestral tetrapod structure. Evolutionary biology explains homologous structures adapted to different purposes as the result of descent with modification from a common ancestor. Homology was explained by Charles Darwin's theory of evolution in 1859, but had been observed before this, from Aristotle onwards, and it was explicitly analysed by Pierre Belon in 1555. The term was applied to biology by the anatomist Richard Owen in 1843.","GENOME101.com
